example.paragraph:
The simplex method could be used here, but the geometric method of Section 9.2 is not too difficult. Graphs of the constraint inequalities (Figure 1) reveal that $\mathcal{F}^{\ast}$ has three extreme points and that $\overline{\mathbf{y}} = \left\lbrack \begin{array}{l}
      \frac{1}{2} \\
      0 \\
      \frac{3}{2}
      \end{array} \right\rbrack$ is the optimal solution. Indeed, $g\text{(}\overline{\mathbf{y}}\text{)} = 30\left( \frac{1}{2} \right) + 20\text{(}0\text{)} + 54\left( \frac{3}{2} \right) = 96\text{,}$ as expected.

content.paragraph:
Notice that the optimal solution to the dual problem appears in the bottom row. The variables $x_{3}$, $x_{4}$, and $x_{5}$ are the slack variables for the first, second, and third equations, respectively. The bottom entry in each of these columns gives the optimal solution $\overline{\mathbf{y}} = \left\lbrack \begin{array}{l}
      \frac{1}{2} \\
      0 \\
      \frac{3}{2}
      \end{array} \right\rbrack$ to the dual problem. This is not a coincidence, as the following theorem shows.

example.paragraph:
so the optimal solution of the dual is $\overline{\mathbf{y}} = \left\lbrack \begin{array}{l}
      \frac{1}{2} \\
      0 \\
      \frac{3}{2}
      \end{array} \right\rbrack$. Thus the marginal value of the cashews is $\frac{1}{2}~$, the marginal value of the filberts is $0$, and the marginal value of the peanuts is $\frac{3}{2}$.﻿

